# LF in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# `text=auto` stores LF whatever the tree holds, so history is safe either way; `eol=lf` settles
# what every tool and script sees, which is the half anybody has to think about. CRLF is not the
# neutral choice here: almost everything that writes a file in this repo is a Node script, the
# formatter or an agent, and all of them emit LF, so a CRLF tree makes each one apply an ending it
# would otherwise never think about. Doing that twice over writes CR CRLF, which stops git
# normalising the file at all, silently — it has happened twice, and
# `c:/dev/knowledge/git-text-and-binary.md` has the mechanism. Nothing tracked here is a `.bat`,
# `.cmd` or `.sh`, so no interpreter has a stake in it. Biome agrees by its own default, and VS
# Code through `files.eol` in `.vscode/settings.json`.
#
# The byte-exact things this repo asserts are unaffected, and it is worth knowing why. The two
# injected lines are string literals with escape sequences rather than literal newlines in source,
# so PATCH_BYTES is 133 whatever this file says. Bundles are read and written as Buffers and never
# through a text encoding (D37), so an extension's own CRLF survives a patch untouched. The corpus
# lives outside the repo and git never sees it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Harvested from the extension bundle, not written by hand. Marked generated so review tools
# collapse them by default, but NOT -diff: these exist to be diffed across extension versions, and
# -diff makes git treat them as binary, which is the one thing they must not be.
#
# At the repo root, not under packages/plugin-api: the table is a consumer's own harvest that
# augments the published package rather than anything the package ships (D40). A path under the
# package would name nothing, which is what this rule did until it was noticed.
generated.ts linguist-generated
